]},
{"clause_id": "c025", "issues": []},
{"clause_id": "c026", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The preservation sentence sits in an Endorsement that in fact changes the Named Insured in Item 1. What \"other than as above stated\" preserves is arguable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.08}
]},
{"clause_id": "c027", "issues": []},
{"clause_id": "c028", "issues": []},
{"clause_id": "c029", "issues": [
  {"issue": "Coverage is withheld unless a Limit of Liability, retention AND premium are \"set forth in the Declarations,\" but Item 6/9 show \"$0\" or \"N/A\" retention for Insuring Agreement (A) and no separate premium for Agreement (B). Whether those Agreements satisfy the tri-partite condition is a conflict with the Declarations table.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
]},
{"clause_id": "c030", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The carve-out \"except for Loss the Company pays as indemnification\" does not say what happens where the Company is legally permitted but unable to indemnify, a gap Section VII (B)(1) addresses differently. The phrase's boundary is the point of construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
]},
{"clause_id": "c031", "issues": [
  {"issue": "\"Has agreed to or is legally permitted or required by law to indemnify\" gives coverage on a permissive-indemnification basis without saying whether actual payment is required. Section VII (B)(2) treats permitted-but-unpaid indemnification as within Insuring Agreement (B), which sharpens the ambiguity.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
]},
{"clause_id": "c032", "issues": [
