]},
{"clause_id": "c043", "issues": [
  {"issue": "\"A written or oral demand for monetary damages or non-monetary relief\" has no threshold of formality, so whether a regulator's or receiver's demand letter or tolling request is a Claim (and when it was \"first made\") is open. The list is also confined to enumerated proceedings, which raises whether informal investigations qualify.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35}
]},
{"clause_id": "c044", "issues": []},
{"clause_id": "c045", "issues": [
  {"issue": "This definition is deleted and replaced by paragraph 4 of the Discovery Period Activation Endorsement, which substitutes a different definition including \"the acquiring entity.\" Which definition of Company governs a Claim first made in the Discovery Period must be worked out between the two.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
]},
{"clause_id": "c046", "issues": [
  {"issue": "\"Reasonable and necessary legal fees and expenses\" is not measured against any benchmark, and Section IX (B)(2) leaves the Insurer to evaluate reasonableness and necessity itself. What is reasonable and necessary is a standing point of construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
]},
{"clause_id": "c047", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The statute is misnamed as the \"Employment Retirement Income Security Act\" rather than the Employee Retirement Income Security Act. The mistake is mechanical but the term drives both the ERISA Exclusion and the Fiduciary Liability Insuring Agreement.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.10}
]},
{"clause_id": "c048", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The definition excludes \"a Leased Employee or Independent Contractor,\" capitalized as defined terms though neither is defined anywhere in Section IV. Who is excluded from \"Employee,\" and therefore from \"Insured Person,\" is left undetermined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
]},
{"clause_id": "c049", "issues": [
  {"issue": "\"Financial Impairment\" is defined as a status \"of the Company\" but the enumerated triggers include the appointment of a receiver over \"the Company or any Subsidiary,\" so it is unclear whether a Subsidiary receivership impairs the Company. The definition is applied elsewhere (Section VII (B), the Discovery Endorsement) to entities other than the Company.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
]},
{"clause_id": "c050", "issues": [
  {"issue": "This definition of Insured is deleted and replaced for Fiduciary Liability purposes by paragraph 2.C of Endorsement 5093, which adds Employee Benefit Plans. Which definition applies to a Claim implicating more than one Insuring Agreement is not addressed.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
]}
]}

